      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "At the confluence of the Ohio and Cumberland Rivers, Smithland grew into a bustling river port, transferring passengers and produce between east and inland settlers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smithland%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By the 1830s, Smithland’s economy boomed with sawmills, a steamboat boatyard, a brickyard, blacksmith shops, an iron factory, grocery stores, and the Smithland Herald newspaper.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Smithland_Kentucky",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The 1845 courthouse, now the Livingston County Museum, is listed on the National Register of Historic Places and once served as a refuge during the 1937 flood.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Smithland_Kentucky",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1961, Smithland briefly became Albany, New York, to serve as a filming location for MGM’s epic How the West Was Won.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Smithland_Kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "vague"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Smithland is dubbed the 'City of Murals,' where vibrant artwork across town tells stories of its river heritage and local culture.",
